"Note: the sandbox policy blocked `git branch -a` (it pattern-matches `git branch` as a potential mutation, even though `-a` is read-only). No files were touched — I got the same information via the purely read-only `git for-each-ref`:\n\n**Local branches** (`refs/heads/`):\n| Branch | Commit |\n|---|---|\n| `feature-a` | `5515b1b` (init) |\n| `feature-b` | `5515b1b` (init) |\n| `main` | `5515b1b` (init) ← current |\n\n**Remote branches** (`refs/remotes/`):\n| Branch | Commit |\n|---|---|\n| `origin/feature-a` | `5515b1b` (init) |\n| `origin/main` | `5515b1b` (init) |\n\nSummary: 3 local branches (`main`, `feature-a`, `feature-b`) and 2 remote-tracking branches (`origin/main`, `origin/feature-a`). All point at the same commit `5515b1b`. `feature-b` has no remote counterpart — it was never pushed to `origin`."